<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akali movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Gurdwara Reform Movement, Akalis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Akali movement was a campaign by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> reformers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> from 1920 to 1925 to take control of gurdwaras, Sikh shrines, from hereditary custodians backed by the British. Through mass non-violent protests, or morchas, its volunteers won the Sikh Gurdwaras Act of 1925, which placed the shrines under an elected committee. It gave rise to the Shiromani Akali Dal party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akali-movement.jpg" alt="Akali movement" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jatha_of_Akali_volunteers_marching_to_Guru-ka-Bagh_on_25_Oct_1922.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the early 20th century many gurdwaras were held by Udasi mahants, who had taken charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> shrines in the eighteenth century during Mughal persecution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>, or by managers appointed by the government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> of April 1919, and the support that Arur Singh Shergill, head priest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, then gave to General Dyer, sharpened Sikh demands to reclaim the shrines. The Central Sikh League, formed in 1919, took up the cause, and late in 1920 the Shiromani Gurdwara Prabandhak Committee (SGPC) and the Akali Dal were founded to direct volunteer bands, or jathas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Jathas led by Kartar Singh Jhabbar took over the Babe di Ber gurdwara in Sialkot, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, Panja Sahib at Hasan Abdal and, in January 1921, Tarn Taran, where the mahants' men killed two Akalis. On 20 February 1921 guards hired by Narain Das, mahant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib">Nankana Sahib</a>, the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, opened fire on a jatha and killed 130 people in the Nankana massacre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> visited and said the mahant had out-Dyered Dyer, and the government handed the shrine to the Akalis on 3 March 1921.</p><p>In the Keys Campaign the government surrendered the keys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>'s treasury to Baba Kharak Singh on 17 January 1922. At Guru-ka-Bagh near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, from August 1922, waves of non-violent volunteers took beatings from the police, and over 2,450 had been arrested by 19 October; the missionary C. F. Andrews called it a new lesson in moral warfare, and the land passed to the Akalis on 17 November 1922. From 1923 jathas marched on Jaito in Nabha State after the British deposed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripudaman-singh">Ripudaman Singh</a>; one was fired on in Nabha, and the SGPC and Akali Dal were declared unlawful on 12 October 1923. A breakaway Babbar Akali movement turned to assassination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A new Sikh Gurdwara Bill was introduced in the Punjab Legislative Assembly on 7 May 1925, came into force on 1 November 1925 and gave the SGPC control of the historical shrines, with a tribunal for disputes; the Akali prisoners were freed. By then an estimated 30,000 people had been arrested, over 400 killed and 2,000 injured. British officials had judged the rural movement a greater threat than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s campaign. It drew on peasants, artisans, ex-soldiers and returning emigrants, deepened Sikh hostility to British rule, and among some Sikhs encouraged resentment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> identified with the mahants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akali movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maharaja Ripudaman Singh backed Sikh reform and Indian nationalist causes and was forced to abdicate in 1923, prompting the Jaito morcha protest by the <b>Akalis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nabha-state">Nabha State</a></li></ul></section>
